<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bolko II of Ziębice</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1300–<a href="/years/1341">1341</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bolko II was a Silesian Piast duke who received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziebice">Ziębice</a> (Münsterberg) when his father Bolko I's lands were divided in 1322, founding it as a separate duchy. He became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> in 1336, like most Silesian dukes, and was succeeded by his son Nicholas the Small.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bolko-ii-of-ziebice.jpg" alt="Bolko II of Ziębice" width="320" height="468" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Theodor Blätterbauer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bolko_II._von_M%C3%BCnsterberg.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bolko II was the fourth son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolko-i-the-strict">Bolko I the Strict</a> and his wife Beatrix, daughter of Otto V the Long, Margrave of Brandenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzwedel">Salzwedel</a>. He was named after his eldest brother Bolko, who had died two days before his birth. When his father died in 1301 he was not yet two years old, and the children were placed in the care of their mother and the guardianship of their uncle Herman, Margrave of Brandenburg-Salzwedel, until 1305, when the eldest surviving brother, Bernard, took over the government.</p><p>For fear that the duchy would be split up, Bolko II was at first meant for a career in the Church. He resisted strongly, and in 1322 Bernard gave him the district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziebice">Ziębice</a> as a duchy of his own. That year the two brothers also joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> on an expedition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Short of money, Bolko II seized goods from the monasteries of Henryków and Kamieniec Ząbkowicki, and in 1329 he attacked the suite of the papal legate Pietro di Alverni. The kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> intervened with the support of almost all the Silesian dukes; Bolko II was defeated and excommunicated by Nanker, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>, until he paid large sums to the legate and the monasteries.</p><p>In 1335 Charles of Luxembourg, Margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> and heir to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziebice">Ziębice</a> because Bolko II refused to pay homage; the duchy lay on the shortest route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>. Bolko II defeated a small force and captured 150 Bohemian knights, asking only a small ransom for them, though he could not prevent Charles's army from laying waste to his lands. Once King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iii-the-great">Casimir III the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> withdrew from the Silesian dukes, Bolko II's hope of resisting Bohemia was gone, and his homage of 1336 meant that the duchy would pass to the Bohemian crown when his male line died out.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His debts, made worse by his lavish spending, forced him in 1337 to pledge Ząbkowice to Charles of Luxembourg and then Strzelin and Kąty Wrocławskie, until he held only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziebice">Ziębice</a> itself; he nonetheless cared for the town's economic growth. By 1321 he had married Bonne de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savoie">Savoie</a>, also called Guta, widow of the Hungarian nobleman Matthew IV Csák, and they had a daughter, Margareta, a nun at Strzelin. He died at Ziębice on 11 June 1341 and was buried at Henryków.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bolko II of Ziębice” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1322 the sons of Duke Bolko I of Świdnica divided his lands, and <b>Bolko II</b> received Ziębice (Münsterberg), south of Wrocław, as a separate duchy split from the Duchy of Świdnica.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-ziebice">Duchy of Ziębice</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziebice">Ziębice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.6,17.04&amp;year=1326" aria-label="Ziębice on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4939 407 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Bolko II of Ziębice"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5389 677h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5389 677h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=50.6,17.04&amp;year=1326">What was here in 1326? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
